/target

# Vendored Python lane cores: bytecode is regenerated on every lane run
__pycache__/

# The generated embedding index beside the fact tree it derives from —
# rebuilt on load, like __pycache__
/memories/.recall-index.json

# Orphans from the atomic rewrites: the index and the recall-count bump
# each write through a unique `.<pid>.<n>.tmp` and rename, so a crash
# mid-write can leave one behind. A leftover temp is never a fact.
/memories/*.tmp

# KI-062's per-fact write locks: a sibling `.lock` beside each fact,
# opened for the whole read-modify-write so a consolidation pass and a
# live recall-count bump cannot lose an update. It is deliberately left
# on disk — unlinking a flocked file another process holds is a race —
# so it is ignored rather than cleaned up. The loader reads `*.md` only,
# so a lock never enters recall.
/memories/*.lock

# The corpus-refresh jobs' state-sync facts: one snapshot per project per
# scheduled window, written through `remember` by the cron lane and
# superseded by the next window's. Runtime state like the index above —
# the run is the record and the fact is its stamp — so they stay on disk
# (recall reads them) but out of the tree. The rules key on the
# SUBSTANCE the ruling named (KI-386): a corpus snapshot carries a year,
# before or after the token — id-before-token, undashed dates, and
# date-before-token all land under one class, so a spelling can never
# decide what gets committed. `seed_task` stamps the canonical shape
# (corpus at the head, a dashed date at the tail) so new snapshots land
# by construction. A durable fact about the corpus is named for its
# claim — no year, no item count — and stays tracked; the one shape
# these rules cannot tell apart is a durable claim that itself names a
# year beside the word corpus, so name such a claim without the year.
/memories/*corpus*20[0-9][0-9]*.md
/memories/*20[0-9][0-9]*corpus*.md
/memories/*corpus*-items*.md

# Claude local env reference — env var names and URLs only, not credential values
CLAUDE.local.md

# Finder / tooling orphans that appear in any working tree
.DS_Store
docs/.DS_Store
.v2c/
.video_agent/

# A plan's generated graph, beside the plan document it executes — the
# execution record, rebuilt from the declaration whenever the plan
# changes. The plan is git-tracked; its runtime state is not.
docs/plans/*.graph.json

# Scratch and harness-side noise, never repo content: tmp/ holds the vm
# harnesses, screenshots and one-off scripts a session verifies with
# (the harness lives in tmp/ and is never committed), and .zcode/ is the
# harness's own plan store for a session, not fleet's.
/tmp/
/.zcode/

# Runtime state the binary writes into whatever directory it runs from:
# the refresh and job runners keep their scratch under .runs/,
# .job-artifacts/ and .corpus-refresh/, and the browser lane writes the
# images a page capture returns to `generated/`. All of it is rebuilt on
# the next run — the home's stores and the tracked docs are the record.
/.runs/
/.job-artifacts/
/.corpus-refresh/
/services/browser/generated/

# Archify's authoring folders: each request keeps its candidate.json and
# render beside the gate evidence that proved it. The diagrams themselves
# ship from docs/architecture/, so the workroom is not repo content.
/.archify/

# Doc locks: the same seam as the memory locks above — fleet's filelock
# leaves a sibling `.lock` beside a document it is editing, and unlinking
# a flocked file another process holds is a race, so they stay on disk
# and out of the tree.
/docs/**/*.lock

# The delegation tests' fixture path ("change home.txt") resolves against
# the process's working directory, so a suite run leaves one at the repo
# root — harness-side noise, the same class the tmp/ rule names.
/home.txt
/home.txt.lock

# The release/version scripts' write locks — the same filelock seam as the
# memory and doc locks (KI-062's shape): runtime state, not repo content.
/scripts/*.lock

# CHANGELOG lock sidecar, same seam
CHANGELOG.md.lock
